Ladybird takes a storage request’s extension identity from the UI
process’s own record of the requesting context, at its current revision,
and requires the storage permission. It sends committed changes to every
live context of the extension, the writer’s included.

Chromium routes writes through its StorageFrontend, and from
OnSettingsChanged(), dispatches storage.onChanged and the area’s
event with EventRouter::DispatchEventToExtension(). Gecko binds
ext-storage’s callMethodInParentProcess() to context.extension, so
the identity comes from the caller’s context as well. WebKit checks the
storage permission in WebExtensionContext::isStorageMessageAllowed(),
where unlimitedStorage alone suffices too; Ladybird requires storage
itself, as Chromium does.
